WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Speaker 1: Donald Trump's ethics lawyer allegedly&nbsp;
told a witness to the January six Trump Riot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:14.220
Committee to lie under oath. This is absolutely&nbsp;
unbelievable. CNN has an exclusive report. Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:21.000
former White House ethics lawyer told Kassidy&nbsp;
Hutchinson to give misleading testimony to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.080
the January 6th committee, according to multiple&nbsp;
sources. Look at this. The January six committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.540
made a startling allegation on Monday, claiming&nbsp;
it had evidence that a Trump backed attorney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.760
urged a key witness to mislead the&nbsp;
committee about details they recalled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:46.260
The committee declined to identify the people.&nbsp;
CNN has learned that Stefan Passantino, the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.540
ethics attorney in the Trump White House, is the&nbsp;
lawyer who allegedly advised his then client,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.740
former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson,&nbsp;
to tell the committee that she did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:01.500
recall details that she did recall. Sources&nbsp;
familiar with the committee's work tell CNN.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.720
Trump's Save America PAC funded Passantino and&nbsp;
his law firm Elections LLC, including paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.700
for his representation of Hutchinson. Other&nbsp;
sources tell CNN the committee report notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.940
the lawyer did not tell his client who was paying&nbsp;
for the legal services. When asked about pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:27.540
on Hutchinson after Monday's hearing, committee&nbsp;
member Representative Zoe Lofgren told CNN she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:34.080
was advised to say she didn't recall something&nbsp;
when she did. So that's pretty serious stuff. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.760
episode is just one of several instances in which&nbsp;
the committee has accused members of Trump's orbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:45.900
of trying to obstruct the panel's investigation.&nbsp;
This is extraordinarily serious. If true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:54.000
if a lawyer were to tell a congressional witness&nbsp;
to lie under oath, it could potentially constitute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.160
the crime of perjury or the related offense of&nbsp;
suborning of perjury or maybe subordination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.400
perjury, which is the act of inducing someone to&nbsp;
commit perjury. And it's important to note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.340
the specific legal consequences of that depend&nbsp;
on the details of the case the applicable law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.240
the circumstances, what you can prove, as&nbsp;
is often the case when it comes to perjury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:25.440
etc., etc.. Now, you and I, of course, recognize&nbsp;
the kind of oxymoronic nature of a Trump ethics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:32.340
lawyer. I get that we're not even going to focus&nbsp;
in on that. But this is sort of like a third layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:39.240
of what is going on with the Trump rights, because&nbsp;
you have sort of at the top of the food chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:46.440
as the case may be, at the top of the pyramid is&nbsp;
the riot itself and who was involved in inciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.220
and the planning of that riot? That's number&nbsp;
one. Number two is you have the witnesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:59.160
at the Trump Riot Committee hearings&nbsp;
and whether they told the truth or lied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:05.400
whether they were tampered with, etc.. The&nbsp;
third layer now is, were there other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:13.620
influencing the testimony of the witnesses at&nbsp;
the January six committee hearing about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.800
Trump riots and who incited those? So you've got&nbsp;
like Trump inciting the riot and then you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:26.580
Cassidy Hutchinson testifying about what she saw&nbsp;
in terms of who was inciting or talking about or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:32.880
trying to dissuade the rioters. And now we have&nbsp;
a third level, which is who influenced Cassidy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.140
Hutchinson's testimony and potentially told&nbsp;
her to say she doesn't recall things that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:42.360
actually does recall about that top layer of the&nbsp;
pyramid. We now have three layers to this pyramid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:48.420
The fourth layer maybe is like the rioters&nbsp;
themselves. In some way it starts to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:56.760
extraordinarily complicated. But this is already&nbsp;
above and beyond anything that we have seen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:02.940
the history of the United States. Part of&nbsp;
the difference is in an era of records for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:09.660
communication and text messages, an event that was&nbsp;
on camera and on and on, that makes it different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:14.040
in terms of our ability to actually see and figure&nbsp;
out what went on. And that's understandable. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.840
there has never been anything like this in&nbsp;
American history. And so we are going to at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:25.500
some point see if and when the Justice Department&nbsp;
decides to act on those criminal referrals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.280
have already been sent forward. And that applies&nbsp;
to Donald Trump, as we learned earlier this week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.160
John Eastman, the lawyer, and others. But there's&nbsp;
this whole other layer, which is what about all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:41.100
of the perjury that is suspected among some of&nbsp;
the witnesses and then who got to them and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.440
there indeed witness tampering? One of the&nbsp;
things that was also alleged during maybe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:53.220
was the second to last or one of the kind of later&nbsp;
public hearings of the January six committee was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.860
that there were individuals there were multiple&nbsp;
instances of individuals reaching out to witnesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.680
and allegedly trying to intimidate or tamper. I&nbsp;
don't believe we ever actually fully got to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:10.080
bottom of that. I don't think this example is the&nbsp;
full scope of that, and that's just another layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.760
that needs to be investigated. Unfortunately, the&nbsp;
January six committee is going to end because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.320
session of Congress is ending and then Republicans&nbsp;
are taking over. They don't want to look into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.700
riots. They want to look into stuff like the&nbsp;
genitalia of Hunter Biden and stuff like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.440
So things are going to change dramatically,&nbsp;
but we will hopefully get to the bottom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:35.040
this component. And again, Trump ethics lawyer,&nbsp;
I don't know of any greater oxymoron than that.

